Consider the C snippet (assuming allocation succeeds):
int *a = (int *)calloc(4, sizeof(int));
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) a[i] += (i + 1) * 3;
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) sum += a[i];
printf("%d", sum);
free(a);
The integer value printed is __________.

explanation:
Primary Derivation: On failure, realloc returns NULL without freeing the old block;
overwriting p loses the only pointer to the old block, causing a memory leak (Option B).
Alternative Verification: Best practice is int *tmp = realloc(p, ...); if (tmp) p = tmp;
confirming Option B.

